WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
The strategy is to ignore the outside. You know, ignore the outside, and the outside is going to ignore me. That's what they're supposed to do.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:24.000
In the Torah, they have something interesting. Now, say in popular culture in America, suppose a person has a drinking problem.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:33.000
So he said, well, no, I'm not an alcoholic. Really, I'm not. I just drink because there was an occasion when I saw my friend.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:45.000
So I'm not really an alcoholic, yes. Now, when they tried to help this person, they said, the first thing you have to admit, you are an alcoholic.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
One that you know that are Catholic, we might be able to help you.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
In David Amenet says something fantastic, says,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Bechattati neti tami. My sin, whatever my faults are, are in front of me.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Now, I am aware of what my problems are. Bechattati neti tami.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:24.000
I'm always looking at that. So the Jewish way really is to confront, not to evade the outer thing.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.000
If there's a problem, yes, the first thing is confront it. And in our community, because we were

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
came children, children of immigrants, we avoided the outside world.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.000
But sooner or later we are going to have to confront it.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:46.000
And that's what I'm trying to say is math is the way to confront the outside world.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
You know, the usurant, the world that is controlled by math, you know.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:13.940
So math is a way to, it's like a passport that opens for you all kind of doors. And the people who are fluent in that kind of language are the people who, the leaders believe in society.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:21.940
And it's a language. Math is no different than English or Chinese. It's a language.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:28.940
And once you are acquainted with the rudiments, you have to know every single word of the language.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.940
You have a dictionary. You open the dictionary and it helps you.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.940
Today we have more than this. We have all the computer things that could help us in a thousand ways.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:43.400
But you cannot afford to ignore the outside world.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.540
And that's why I try to convince people.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.540
What's going on in Israel?

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.020
I have, in a way, something, any bombs?

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.100
There was a bomb in the Nahrimia train station.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.040
Yes? When?

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.300
This morning.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.060
This morning?

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:13.060
And two people were killed on the closest Jordanian border. Teachers. One teacher and the driver of the van.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.060
Again, the problem with Israel is exactly what I'm saying.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:25.060
Try always to confront the problem by saying that the enemy really is not bad, or this is not.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.060
They really didn't mean that, you know.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:36.060
A lot of what happened to us six years ago, that, well, Hitler really, when they said,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.060
we want to exterminate the Jews, we don't mean it.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.060
They don't, they could not possibly mean that, you know.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:54.060
And this escapism, which is typical of certain structures of thought, it's not helpful.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:04:05.060
If I have somebody who wants to kill me, there are many ways to cope with the problem, but one way not to is to say, well, no, really he doesn't want to kill me.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:22.060
He does not. It's basically a good problem. And that is what Israel today is doing. I mean, not today, 75 years ago, they were doing the problem.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:28.060
They don't understand, they're very politically thick-minded.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:39.060
Maybe you wish you could all leave Israel and go to New Zealand, or one of the islands around Australia, or whatever it is.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:48.060
But you cannot solve the problem by pretending that your enemy is not your enemy.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:05:02.060
And I have sometimes, you know, I'm not going to say father because nobody's eating, but somebody should eat and this and this.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.060
We'll speak about it after breakfast.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:24.800
No, no, no. I showed that when David Nassi came to America, he went to Philadelphia.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:33.800
And he was the first Jew in America to have a license to practice medicine and to practice,

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.040
and he was a pharmacist. He had a pharmacy.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.340
So he's a close person.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:45.400
And he was a good friend, close friend of Benjamin Franklin.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.820
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.440
Good connection.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:55.960
Get me something.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:57.600
A white fish?

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:58.120
Yeah.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:26.180
Thank you.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.120
and Baal HaTabai, the uncle of Baal HaTabai, Shari'in,

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:40.120
Wal Banach, from Hassan, and Abu Sarif, from Syria.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.120
Now...

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.120
Pass. Pass.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:44.120
Pass. Hurry.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:47.120
Alright.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.120
We have a... really, the Torah system is a unique system.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.120
And that's why I'm against talking about Torah and religion.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.840
Because religion is a concept that after Christianity,

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.840
and why should I try to explain my Torah in Christian terms?

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.240
I mean, why should I?

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.080
It's something different.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.080
Just like you say, you know, like I, I don't know,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.840
I never saw a human being,

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.120
and, but I know I've seen a lot of monkeys.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.340
You've got to explain human anatomy according to baboons.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:25.340
I mean, you know, why, we have our own structure, you know.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.140
We're different, we're not better or worse, but we're different.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:37.640
Now, the central idea of Torah is a covenant that we have with God in Sinai.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:41.840
We have the covenant. God proposes something for us.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:44.140
Do you want it?

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.640
You say yes.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:53.040
If you pay attention in yesterday's parashah, it's also this covenant.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.040
and got me the carpet.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.040
And that carpet, unlike the carpet in Sinai,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:03.040
there's a whole list of pluses and minuses.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:05.040
There are 14 kerevot of them.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.040
There's pluses and there's minuses.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.040
And at the end we say, yes,

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.040
and there's the belittin of Moab.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.040
And this parashah, this week's parashah,

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.040
begins with,

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:31.540
Now, you are all standing, and I'll explain in a minute the word Meshav, you're standing today in front of God.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:35.420
And I'll explain to you what it means in front of God, how to be in front of God.

00:08:35.420 --> 00:09:01.680
and says everything, your judges, your police, police force, the men, the women, the children, the servants, and we ascertain the covenant.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:11.680
The last covenant we had in the last weeks of Passover. What does it mean? What's the purpose of the second covenant? What's the meaning of it?

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:24.680
There's a simple thing, a simple idea that we have forgotten. This idea. Sometimes we take things for granted and we don't teach it. And then we forget about it.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:54.680
We have two governments because we have two dimensions in Judaism. First is my obligation, personal obligation to God. Yes, not in love, not in sorrow, not in art, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love, not in love

00:09:54.680 --> 00:10:04.680
meaning God. And therefore if there are punishments, there will be punishments, or rewards, they're not mentioned there. Why?

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:15.680
Because at the personal level, the sikhah is in honor of God. The reward is not in this world, essentially.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:21.680
And the punishments are not essentially in this world. It's a personal thing, a power between me and God.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.640
Zahor T'moshabbat, Shomol T'moshabbat,

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:30.480
I try to keep this up. What are the consequences of doing it or not doing it?

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:33.920
That is personal thing between me and God.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.920
But then there is another thing.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:41.920
We have a covenant as a nation, a national.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:45.920
In Moab, that's the last of the Spersha,

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:51.440
before we enter to the land of Israel,

00:10:51.440 --> 00:11:09.680
We make the covenant not as individuals because we are already made, but as a nation. We are the nation of Israel. We have, I have the only word that I have is English because we don't have any other terms.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:34.720
It's a political dimension. Political in the sense that it's a national, it's a country. Not a country based upon land, but it's a country based upon certain commitments, not upon ourselves. This commitment, yes, really if you want to translate the word Torah in its proper sense into English, we have to do it like the Jews did two thousand years ago in Egypt.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:39.720
In Egypt, the Jews spoke in a Greek, and they wanted to say the word Torah,

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:45.720
they translated the word Torah as nomos, which means the constitution, it's the constitution of the Jewish people,

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:52.720
this is our constitution. The nation of Israel has, and is based upon, the constitution.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:12:01.720
Later on they're going to cut the sections, yes? But for us Jews, our Torah is not less sacred than the constitution of the U.S.A.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:08.220
USA is for an American, you know, or, or, or, or, or, or, you know, or any other country, you know, the

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:15.720
, this is our constitution. And as a constitution, here comes the beautiful

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:27.220
thing of this . This binds you as a, a, a, a, a, a nation in a general way.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:32.420
I have to do it whether I want it or not because this is the way the Jewish nation is doing it.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:37.960
The whole idea of a Harer HaBibliya talk, and the whole idea that Al-Kifroshina is a book,

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:44.320
do not withdraw, do not break away from the community,

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.400
is because we have, besides our personal thing, we should have our personal thing,

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:55.140
you know, between ourselves and God, we have commitments as a member of a nation.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.140
the nation of Israel.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:00.140
And you all serve today before God.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.140
That was also the question.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:04.140
Because with the covenant,

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:06.140
the one who is here,

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:08.140
and the one who is not here,

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.140
is the one who believes today.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:11.140
What does it mean?

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.140
When America,

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:18.140
USA makes an agreement with France,

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:23.140
the agreement is not with the specific person

